@meta
id: 13-05
title: Anything but
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-classes
par: 39
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F for status=, Enter through nine matches, stop at the odd ones
open: gateway.log

@brief
Every gateway response in this log carries a `status=` code. Most are
`2xx`. The ones that are not — whatever they are — need ` alert=true`
appended so the alerting job picks them up on replay.

You do not know the failing codes in advance. A `502` you can guess; a
`404` and a `503` are also in there, and next week it will be something
else. What you can describe is the shape: `status=` followed by a digit
that is *not* a `2`.

Brackets build a set. A caret as the first thing inside the brackets
turns the set inside out.

@keys
| `[^2]` | any one character that is not a `2` |
| `[^0-9]` | any one character that is not a digit — the same as `\D` |
| `[^,]` | anything but a comma, which is how you match "one CSV field" |
| `n` then `.` | next match, repeat the edit |

@hints
Say what the bad lines have in common rather than listing them. It is
the digit straight after `status=`.
--
Inside brackets, a leading `^` means "not one of these". `[^2]` is any
character except `2`, so `status=[^2]` lands on the non-2xx responses
and skips the healthy ones.
--
`/status=[^2]` `Enter`, `A alert=true`, `Esc`. Then `n.` for each
remaining match — there are three lines altogether.

@solution
/status=[^2]<Enter>  A alert=true<Esc>  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-four keystrokes for three lines, and the pattern would have
found thirty.

`[^...]` is the negated set, and it is the most useful bracket
expression there is, because most of the time what you know about the
text is a boundary rather than the content. "Everything up to the next
comma" is `[^,]*`. "Everything inside the quotes" is `'[^']*'`. "A word
that is not followed by a bracket" starts with a negated set. You will
see `[^,]*` again before the chapter is out, doing exactly that job on
a CSV.

Two things to watch. The caret only negates when it is the *first*
character inside the brackets — `[a^]` is a set containing `a` and `^`.
And a negated set still matches exactly one character, so
`status=[^2]` needs *something* after the `=`; it would not match
`status=` at the end of a line. When you want "not a 2, or nothing at
all", you need a quantifier, which is a few lessons away.

The `n.` pair is chapter 11's. The pattern found the right lines; `.`
did the same append on each. A pattern that describes the *shape* of a
problem, plus a repeatable edit, is a find-and-replace that you review
one match at a time.

